1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:06,000
Welcome to the ERM platform demonstration series.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Over the next several minutes, you'll watch a real risk move through governed approval

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:17,000
from a Risk Manager's first submission to a Chief Risk Officer's final sign-off.

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:23,000
This is the pattern that governs every risk, control, incident, and audit finding across this platform.

5
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Every regulated financial institution carries risk it cannot fully eliminate:

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,000
credit risk, operational risk, technology risk, third-party risk.

7
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:37,000
What separates a well-governed institution isn't the absence of risk —

8
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:43,000
it's whether that risk is identified, scored, owned, and reviewed on a disciplined cycle,

9
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:52,000
and whether every change to that picture can be defended to a board, a regulator, or an auditor after the fact.

10
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:55,000
That discipline starts with a risk register —

11
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:01,000
a single, structured system of record where every identified risk has an owner, a category,

12
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:05,000
a documented likelihood and impact score, and a scheduled review date.

13
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:11,000
Today you'll see that register inside a real, working application — not a slide.

14
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:14,000
But a register on its own isn't governance —

15
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:17,000
anyone could edit a score and walk away.

16
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:19,000
Real governance requires separation of duties:

17
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:24,000
the person who proposes a change to a risk's rating, the maker,

18
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,000
can never be the same person who approves it, the checker.

19
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:32,000
This maker-checker control is a cornerstone requirement for regulated entities —

20
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:40,000
including SEBI-regulated Mutual Fund Asset Management Companies, the primary market this platform is built for today.

21
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:43,000
This platform is built on two layers.

22
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:47,000
PRSMTD is the underlying governance substrate: multi-tenancy, role-based access,

23
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:52,000
and — critically — an append-only governance ledger that enforces maker-checker wherever it's needed

24
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:55,000
and records every decision permanently for audit.

25
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:00,000
ERM is the specification and module layer built on top of it:

26
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:05,000
Risk, Controls, Compliance, Audit, Policy, Incident and CAPA, Third-Party Risk, and more —

27
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:10,000
each one reusing that same governance engine rather than inventing its own.

28
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:16,000
In this first demonstration, you'll watch the canonical example of that pattern end to end:

29
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:20,000
a Risk Manager submits a periodic re-assessment on an active risk,

30
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:27,000
and a Chief Risk Officer — a different person, holding a different role — reviews and decides it.

31
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:31,000
Watch for three things: how separation of duties is enforced automatically,

32
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:34,000
how the risk's status changes at each step,

33
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:38,000
and how a complete, timestamped decision trail is left behind

34
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:44,000
for anyone who later needs to answer: who approved this, and why.

35
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:59,000
The platform you're about to see is populated with a fictional but realistic tenant:

36
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:07,000
Meridian Asset Management Limited, a SEBI-regulated mutual fund AMC.

37
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:17,000
Every persona, risk, and record you'll see belongs to this one illustrative organization.

38
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:20,000
Two people carry this workflow.

39
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:27,000
Arjun Mehta is Meridian's Risk Manager, sitting in the Risk Management department.

40
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:35,000
His job is to keep the risk register current — identifying risks, running periodic re-assessments,

41
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:41,000
and proposing changes to a risk's likelihood, impact, or status.

42
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:47,000
He is today's maker: the person who proposes the change.

43
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:51,000
Priya Raghunathan is Meridian's Chief Risk Officer.

44
00:03:51,000 --> 00:04:01,000
She owns the independent risk management function and holds the checker role for the Risk module —

45
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:09,000
meaning any governed change Arjun proposes must pass across her desk before it takes effect.

46
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:17,000
She is today's checker: the person who decides.

47
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:26,000
Notice that Arjun and Priya sit in the same department but hold different roles.

48
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:32,000
That's deliberate — separation of duties is about role, not department,

49
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:35,000
and the platform enforces it structurally, not just as a formality.

50
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:43,000
Arjun signs in and lands on the Risk Register —

51
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:52,000
the enterprise-wide list of every identified risk at Meridian, spanning fund management, operations, customer service, and more.

52
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:59,000
This is the single source of truth the rest of the organization works from:

53
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:08,000
risk owners, department heads, and the CRO all read from this same list rather than separate spreadsheets.

54
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:17,000
Each row shows a risk code, its title, category, current status, and its inherent and residual score —

55
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:20,000
the two numbers that drive prioritization.

56
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:27,000
Arjun opens RSK-2026-0004: ‘Credit default of a portfolio issuer below investment grade’ —

57
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:33,000
a credit risk owned by the Investment Management function, currently Active,

58
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:39,000
with an inherent score of ten and a residual score of eight

59
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:48,000
after existing controls are taken into account.

60
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:54,000
Notice the detail screen's action bar: Submit Re-assessment, Escalate, Propose Risk Acceptance, Propose Retirement.

61
00:05:54,000 --> 00:06:01,000
These are the governed actions available to Arjun in this risk's current status —

62
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:07,000
and only because no other action is already pending against this record.

63
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:18,000
That constraint matters, and you'll see why in a moment.

64
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:30,000
This risk's last assessment was in June, with its next review due in September.

65
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:34,000
Rather than wait, Arjun has fresh information —

66
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:40,000
a rating agency has revised its outlook on the issuer —

67
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:51,000
and he's initiating a periodic re-assessment now.

68
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:53,000
He clicks Submit Re-assessment.

69
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:59,000
This is a governed action: the button itself tells him so,

70
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:06,000
warning that once submitted, no further changes are allowed until a checker decides.

71
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:10,000
A justification is mandatory — free text won't do;

72
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:21,000
this is the evidentiary record an auditor will read later.

73
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:28,000
Arjun writes his rationale: credit spreads have widened following a one-notch outlook revision,

74
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:32,000
but the segregated-portfolio trigger has not been breached,

75
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:38,000
and existing controls and heightened monitoring continue to operate effectively.

76
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:43,000
He confirms the residual likelihood and impact are unchanged.

77
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:51,000
This is the maker's case — the reasoning a checker will independently evaluate, not simply rubber-stamp.

78
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:57,000
Arjun clicks ‘Submit for approval.’

79
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:02,000
Watch the status badge: Active becomes Under Review,

80
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:12,000
and a second badge appears — Awaiting checker.

81
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:15,000
This is the governance ledger at work.

82
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:22,000
Behind the scenes, the platform has created a pending action record, linked to this risk,

83
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:29,000
and — critically — it will allow exactly one pending action per record at a time.

84
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:33,000
This is a rule the platform calls GOV-07,

85
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:42,000
and you can see its effect immediately: every action button that was available a moment ago has disappeared.

86
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:49,000
Arjun cannot submit anything further against this risk, and he cannot approve his own submission.

87
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:52,000
The system tells him so directly: separation of duties requires a different checker to decide it.

88
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:58,000
The risk is now frozen in a governed, in-flight state

89
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:06,000
until someone with the right authority makes a decision.

90
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:12,000
This is the moment worth pausing on,

91
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:21,000
because it's the heart of enterprise governance: maker-checker control.

92
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:25,000
Why does separation of duties exist at all?

93
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:32,000
Because a single person acting alone — however well-intentioned — is a single point of failure.

94
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:34,000
Errors go uncaught.

95
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:41,000
Pressure to hit a number, close a quarter, or avoid an uncomfortable conversation

96
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:44,000
can quietly bias a self-approved decision.

97
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:48,000
Regulators require independent review of risk management activity

98
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:55,000
precisely because self-review cannot be trusted to catch what an independent reviewer will.

99
00:09:55,000 --> 00:10:04,000
The platform enforces this structurally: the same rule that blocked Arjun from deciding his own submission a moment ago

100
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:09,000
would block anyone, on any record, in any module built on this governance ledger.

101
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:15,000
We now switch personas to Priya Raghunathan, Meridian's Chief Risk Officer —

102
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:18,000
the checker you met earlier.

103
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:25,000
Because she holds the checker role and did not create this submission,

104
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:32,000
the platform grants her exactly the authority Arjun didn't have a moment ago:

105
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:39,000
the ability to decide it.

106
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:43,000
Priya opens Approvals — a single, cross-module queue

107
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:52,000
that surfaces every pending action waiting on a role she holds, regardless of which module it came from.

108
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:56,000
Her queue already carries several other pending items;

109
00:10:56,000 --> 00:11:01,000
Arjun's new submission has landed at the top.

110
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:06,000
This is what auditors value most about this design:

111
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:14,000
every governed decision, across every module, flows through one traceable queue and one ledger —

112
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:21,000
nothing is approved by side channel, email, or verbal sign-off.

113
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:30,000
On the pending item, Priya can see exactly what Arjun submitted:

114
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:34,000
his justification, in full, with a timestamp.

115
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:40,000
She has three options: Approve, Reject, or Return for rework —

116
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:48,000
this last option sends it back to Arjun with feedback rather than forcing an outright rejection,

117
00:11:48,000 --> 00:12:00,000
useful when the case is close but incomplete.

118
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:02,000
Priya clicks Approve.

119
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:07,000
A decision dialog opens, asking for an optional comment —

120
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:14,000
optional here because approval doesn't require justification the way a rejection or a return does,

121
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:18,000
though a disciplined checker adds one anyway.

122
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:22,000
Priya writes that she reviewed the credit-spread data,

123
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:26,000
confirmed the segregated-portfolio trigger has not been breached,

124
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:30,000
and confirms the existing control remains effective.

125
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:36,000
She concurs with the re-assessment.

126
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:38,000
She clicks ‘Confirm approve.’

127
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:43,000
This is the moment the governance ledger closes the loop:

128
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:46,000
the pending action is marked decided,

129
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:51,000
the platform's projection logic updates the underlying risk record,

130
00:12:51,000 --> 00:13:00,000
and both the maker's justification and the checker's decision comment are written permanently to the record's history.

131
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:06,000
Arjun will also receive a notification that his submission was decided —

132
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:10,000
closing the loop back to the person who started it.

133
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:16,000
Back on the risk record's Approvals tab,

134
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:22,000
the full decision is now visible as a single, permanent entry:

135
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:24,000
Risk Reassessment — Approved.

136
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:28,000
Maker: Arjun Mehta, Risk Manager, with his timestamp.

137
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:32,000
Checker: Priya Raghunathan, Chief Risk Officer, with hers.

138
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:45,000
Both the original justification and the final decision comment are preserved verbatim, side by side.

139
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:50,000
This is what an internal auditor or a SEBI inspection would actually look for:

140
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:55,000
not just that a risk score changed, but who proposed the change, why,

141
00:13:55,000 --> 00:14:00,000
who independently reviewed it, what they said, and exactly when each step happened.

142
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:03,000
Nothing here can be edited after the fact —

143
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:08,000
the governance ledger this platform is built on is append-only by design.

144
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:12,000
A new correction is a new entry, never a silent overwrite.

145
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:21,000
That immutability is what turns a risk register from a spreadsheet anyone could quietly edit into a defensible system of record.

146
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:28,000
The risk itself is back to Active —

147
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:34,000
re-assessment doesn't necessarily mean the picture changed, and here it didn't:

148
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:38,000
residual likelihood and impact were reconfirmed, not revised.

149
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:42,000
What changed is something just as important:

150
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:49,000
the organization now has fresh, independently reviewed evidence that this risk is still correctly scored,

151
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:56,000
current as of today rather than a stale June assessment, with a documented trail proving the review actually happened.

152
00:14:56,000 --> 00:15:01,000
That's the real business outcome of a maker-checker workflow.

153
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:05,000
It's not paperwork for its own sake —

154
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:11,000
it's the difference between an organization that can say ‘we manage this risk’

155
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:20,000
and one that can prove it, on demand, to a board committee or a regulator, months or years later.

156
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:26,000
You've just watched the platform's most fundamental governance pattern end to end:

157
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:30,000
a maker proposes, a checker independently decides,

158
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:34,000
and every step is captured permanently for audit.

159
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:39,000
This same pattern — not a different one per module —

160
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:46,000
is what will govern control testing, compliance assessments, audit findings, policy publication, incident closure,

161
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:54,000
and every other approval across this platform, because it all runs on the same governance ledger you saw today.

162
00:15:54,000 --> 00:16:01,000
For a Board or CRO, this is the assurance that risk data is trustworthy.

163
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:09,000
For a CISO or Compliance Officer, this is a control you can point to in an audit.

164
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:17,000
For an implementation team, this is one governance engine to build once and reuse everywhere,

165
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:26,000
not a bespoke approval flow per module.

166
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:29,000
This concludes Demonstration 01.

167
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:35,000
Future demonstrations in this series will walk through control exception handling,

168
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:39,000
compliance assessment approval, and audit finding closure —

169
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:50,000
each one exercising this same governed pattern in a different part of the platform.
